      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 40 and 48, we can use the prime factorization method. Let's start by finding the prime factorizations of each number.\n\n**Prime Factorization of 40:**\n1. 40 is divisible by 2: \\(40 \\div 2 = 20\\)\n2. 20 is divisible by 2: \\(20 \\div 2 = 10\\)\n3. 10 is divisible by 2: \\(10 \\div 2 = 5\\)\n4. 5 is a prime number.\n\nSo, the prime factorization of 40 is:\n\\[ 40 = 2^3 \\times 5 \\]\n\n**Prime Factorization of 48:**\n1. 48 is divisible by 2: \\(48 \\div 2 = 24\\)\n2. 24 is divisible by 2: \\(24 \\div 2 = 12\\)\n3. 12 is divisible by 2: \\(12 \\div 2 = 6\\)\n4. 6 is divisible by 2: \\(6 \\div 2 = 3\\)\n5. 3 is a prime number.\n\nSo, the prime factorization of 48 is:\n\\[ 48 = 2^4 \\times 3 \\]\n\nNext, we identify the common prime factors and their lowest powers in both factorizations:\n- The prime factor \\(2\\) appears in both factorizations.\n- The lowest power of \\(2\\) common to both is \\(2^3\\).\n\nThus, the greatest common factor is:\n\\[ \\text{GCF} = 2^3 = 8 \\]\n\nTherefore, the greatest common factor of 40 and 48 is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
