      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 40 and 48, we can use the prime factorization method. Here are the steps:\n\n1. **Find the prime factorization of each number:**\n\n   - **Prime factorization of 40:**\n     \\[\n     40 \\div 2 = 20 \\\\\n     20 \\div 2 = 10 \\\\\n     10 \\div 2 = 5 \\\\\n     5 \\div 5 = 1\n     \\]\n     So, the prime factorization of 40 is:\n     \\[\n     40 = 2^3 \\times 5\n     \\]\n\n   - **Prime factorization of 48:**\n     \\[\n     48 \\div 2 = 24 \\\\\n     24 \\div 2 = 12 \\\\\n     12 \\div 2 = 6 \\\\\n     6 \\div 2 = 3 \\\\\n     3 \\div 3 = 1\n     \\]\n     So, the prime factorization of 48 is:\n     \\[\n     48 = 2^4 \\times 3\n     \\]\n\n2. **Identify the common prime factors and their lowest powers:**\n   - The prime factors of 40 are \\(2^3\\) and \\(5\\).\n   - The prime factors of 48 are \\(2^4\\) and \\(3\\).\n   - The common prime factor is \\(2\\), and the lowest power of \\(2\\) in both factorizations is \\(2^3\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the GCF:**\n   - The GCF is the product of the lowest powers of all common prime factors.\n   - Here, the common prime factor is \\(2\\) with the lowest power \\(2^3\\).\n   - Therefore, the GCF is:\n     \\[\n     2^3 = 8\n     \\]\n\nSo, the greatest common factor of 40 and 48 is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
